tritious choices  and i love that she doesn't describe  them as foods that she eats because  they're  you know super clean or low in calories  or detoxing whatever  i mean even the cauliflower crust pizza  she eats  it's like she eats them just because  they're easy and they taste pretty good  to her  but also i'm totally stealing that idea  i actually  think that a brand sent me these same  little cauliflower bread things  um and i love that they had some protein  and fiber in them  and also they actually had more flavor  than just like a regular piece of pita  which i use all the time to make little  pita pizzas  anyone else like pita pizzas  so great such an easy lunch or dinner  option  to use up little pieces of veg that you  have left over in the fridge  and her version is so much more fancy  with like the pesto and the sausage and  the nooch  but i also bet that they're really  satisfying and pretty delicious too  i also love those mary's gone crackers  they've got some fiber and protein and  healthy fats in there  and paired with the hummus that would be  a super satisfying  and tasty snack and the peanut butter  and plums  can't go wrong with either of those all  on their lonesome  but of course you could pair them  together for an even more balanced  and satiating snack ooh a little toast  with peanut butter and sliced plum on  top  that would be really good let's see what  else she has to say  so recently i've been drinking iced  herbal tea another weird drink though  that i have is sparkling water with  apple cider vinegar in it it helps with  my digestion  and i have really shitty digestion so  but i'd hate soda  i don't like sweet drinks okay so i  also have been into making iced herbal  tea as  i also try not to have caffeine after  like 10 a.m hashtag insomniac  my cutoff is probably a lot more  conservative than emma's um but anyways  as for the apple cider vinegar this is  actually the first  kind of wellness-y ritual that emma  seems to have in her day  even though she admittedly thinks it's a  bit weird  um so that just says a lot right there  but interestingly  like when emma says that she takes it to  help with her digestion  i just feel like that is an honest  observation  on her part as for the claim itself it's  most likely that the increase in water  intake from this drink  is what helps improve her digestion and  move things along  more so than the apple cider vinegar  specifically  as there is no strong research to show  that apple cider vinegar can help  with bloating and digestion in fact one  small study  looked at the effect of apple cider  vinegar in people with gastroparesis  which is a condition that impairs the  movement of food from the stomach into  the intestines  and the study found that apple cider  vinegar could actually slow  down digestion rather than speeding it  up  which can actually lead to increased  bloating and gas  particularly in individuals with these  types of gi disorders  however people who don't have gi  dysfunction may find that apple cider  vinegar can help their digestion  but these claims are largely anecdotal  so if you like the flavor  like emma does then i think it's a great  way to help you up your water intake in  the day  much better than taking like a shot of  the stuff which can be  a lot more aggressive on your stomach  and your teeth  let's talk dinner dinner time if i'm  cooking for myself  it's usually something pretty low  maintenance i'll take like some frozen  vegetables of some sort heat those up  cut up some tofu fry that i will also  make pasta i love garbanzo bean pasta  and i put pesto on it in nutritional  yeast and i'll eat a whole bowl of that  and call it a night  i love how easy and simple  she makes meal prep this totally sounds  like something that  most people could easily put together  pretty inexpensively  and really quickly so we've got some  protein in the tofu  carbs in the brown rice that's in the  curry sauce and sesame oil  and lots of veg and there's basically no  prep work  involved i also love that she's a fan of  frozen veg because  frozen vegetables and produce is not  only really affordable convenient  and has a longer shelf life than fresh  vegetables  but it's a great way to enjoy your  favorite veggies year round regardless  of whether or not they're in season  and you don't have to worry about them  being significantly less healthy there's  also some research to show that some  frozen veggies like broccoli and kale  may actually be more nutrient dense than  fresh veggies  because they're flash frozen right after  they're harvested  to lock in some of that nutrition now if  they're blanched before freezing  then yes that can reduce some of the  nutrients in some veggies  but in general i think whatever little  time  and money saving hacks help you get in  more vegetables  that is a more important consideration  than  the fresh versus frozen debate speaking  of frozen veggies though  if she did want to boost up the veg in  her pesto pasta  she could definitely throw in some  frozen peas or  kale or if the pesto is made from  scratch she could add some spinach  or kale to that as well either way i  love that she's using bonza pasta and  nutritional yeast for some added protein  these are actually two of my personal  favorite protein hacks in pasta dishes  as well  especially when i'm in a rush and i  don't have time to cook a whole other  protein let's see what else yesterday  quite a few  different kind of comfort meals number  one  would have to be monty's burgers  i also love falafel falafel wrap  with hummus and tahini on it and like  pita bread with the hummus  also burritos like from a really good  place also i love breakfast potatoes so  like sometimes  i just want to order just that okay  kudos to harper's bazaar for replacing  the verbiage about cheat meals with  comfort foods instead you probably know  i'm not a fan of the word cheat meals  and while even comfort food has been  often hijacked by diet culture to be  synonymous with  bad foods or junk foods it is worth  noting that the term  can also just be foods that bring you  joy  no moral slant needed as for emma's  comfort meals  i love seeing her excitement about food  i mean as a self-professed picky eater i  do  think that her comfort meals actually  offer some really good variety  from different cuisines and are also all  great well-rounded vegetarian options  as well so of course we've got the  standard veggie burger which  in my opinion a good burger comforts any  and  every soul on the planet so i think we  can all relate right there  the falafel wrap is also an amazing  option  and offers some really good plant-based  protein and healthy fats from the hummus  and tahini  and then of course a good burrito always  hits the spot  um i think burritos are also pretty  inherently well balanced and definitely  super satiating and if she or you want  to make these comfort foods  everyday foods all i would suggest is  adding in more veg i mean i am all about  small little tweaks and additions  rather than omissions to make cheat  meals  into everyday balanced meals that are  just  equally as comforting now overall emma  chamberlain  not only seems super down-to-earth and  refreshing  but her approach to food and eating is  totally something i can get on board  with  unlike a lot of these celebrity food  diaries we've seen so far which  tend to be rife with misinformation  around food  and wellness culture but i love that  emma  totally marches to the beat of her own  drum and despite her growing popularity  in the youtube space she keeps it real  and  really relatable i am officially  a fan and on that note thank you again  to ritual for sponsoring this video  if you liked it and want to see me  review more of these harper's bizarre  clips  please like this video and leave me a  comment below on who to check out next  until then don't forget to subscribe to  the channel and ring that little bell so  that you never miss out on a video  and i will see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye
 

